cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "Donghyunzam (동현잠): a single-player pencil-sketch submarine combat laboratory, with the number of submarines, the starting stage, the paper colour and the starting bomb stock set through the esoteric language Donghyun-lang"
type: software
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24501338"
version: "0.01"
date-released: "2026-11-24"
authors:
  - family-names: Kim
    given-names: Dong Hyun
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9883-0229"
    affiliation: "Inha University College of Medicine"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  Donghyunzam is a browser-based, noncommercial toy: a side-view submarine
  steered with the directional keys through seven horizontally scrolling
  undersea stages. It shares the frame of its sibling Donghyunchop (seven
  long stages, a 400x225 view, pencil rendering, instrument panel, save
  and load) and replaces the flight model with water drag: speed builds
  slowly and the boat coasts a long way. The primary weapon is the torpedo
  (two tubes; it sinks briefly, then accelerates), and the bomb stock (up
  to ten) is spent on either a mine dropped from the keel or an anti-ship
  missile launched from the deck that breaks the surface and homes on
  frigates, destroyers, Aegis ships and aircraft. The top of the screen is
  a rippling waterline; warships drop depth charges fused to the depth the
  submarine had when they were released. Each stage has a mid-boss and a
  boss - crabs, octopuses, squids, frogs, a newt, a nautilus and a kraken -
  which, besides shooting, strike directly with arms, tongues and claws
  that are redrawn every step as quick pencil strokes after a short
  telegraph. The number of submarines (1-9), the starting stage (1-7), the
  paper colour (rough cream or sketchbook white) and the starting bomb
  stock (0-10) are chosen through a program written in the esoteric
  programming language Donghyun-lang. Input is keyboard, an on-screen
  directional pad with buttons, and gamepad, with fullscreen. A run can be
  saved to three browser slots or to a .dhsave file and loaded again.
  The one image file is the title on the briefing screen, written by hand
  by the author; every vessel, creature, terrain and the paper grain is
  drawn by code as wobbly graphite strokes when the page opens, in
  greyscale on paper, using the pencil engine of Donghyuns. Clearing all
  seven stages is celebrated with falling petals and a short fanfare
  synthesised in the browser from the trio melody of Elgar's Pomp and
  Circumstance March No. 1 (1901, public domain); no recording is used.
  No graphics, sound, text, stage data or code of any other game is used.
  The MIT license applies to the Donghyunzam code and documentation; the
  page fonts are under the SIL Open Font License, and the handwritten
  title remains the work of its writer. See LICENSE and CREDITS.md.
keywords:
  - submarine game
  - side-scrolling
  - procedural drawing
  - esoteric programming language
  - JavaScript
